# 2. Data types

HQL is a statically typed expression and query language. Its central idea is
that a type is a contract. A type describes a value: the fields of a record,
the type of each field, and any rules the values must follow. HQL checks
values against their types at two points.

The first is before the program runs. HQL has no compiler, but `hql`
type-checks the whole program first, the way a compiler would. It infers the
type of every expression and rejects the program if anything does not fit.
This is what makes HQL statically typed.

The second is when data comes in. When a program reads a file, an event or a
row as some type, `hql` validates it against that type and rejects anything
that does not match. Values are never converted implicitly. This is what
makes HQL strongly typed.

A query language needs both. Static checking finds mistakes in the program,
but it cannot see the data, which does not exist yet. Validation finds bad
data, but on its own it reports a mistake in the program only when some data
happens to trigger it.

## Types as data contracts

On a data platform, a data contract is an agreement between the team that
produces a table and the teams that consume it. It lists the columns, their
types, and rules such as "an amount is never negative". It usually lives
outside the code, in a schema registry or a validation job.

In HQL, the contract is the type:

```hql
type BookData : DataContract = {
    title: String
    pages: Int

    require pages > 0
}
```

The checker uses `BookData` to check the query, and the same declaration
validates the rows when the program runs.

## Checking the program

Here the program asks for `page`, but the field is called `pages`:

```hql
type BookData : DataContract = {
    title: String
    pages: Int
}

raw : List<Data> = [{title: "Dune", pages: 412}]
raw | BookData | map(b => b.page / 2)
```

```text
check error[unknown-field]: BookData has no field `page`
 --> <expression>:7:27
  |
7 | raw | BookData | map(b => b.page / 2)
  |                           ^^^^^^
```

This is a `check error`. The checker found it before anything ran, so it does
not matter how many books there are.

## Validating the data

Data from outside arrives as `Data`, a tree of records, lists and scalars,
like JSON or YAML. `Data` is a type, but it does not say which fields are
there. Reading it as a contract does:

```hql
type BookData : DataContract = {
    title: String
    pages: Int

    require pages > 0
}

raw : List<Data> = [{title: "Dune", pages: 412}, {title: "Emma", pages: "many"}]
raw | BookData | map(b => b.pages)
```

```text
execute error[contract-violation]: this does not read as BookData: field `pages`: "many" is not Int
 --> <expression>:9:1
  |
9 | raw | BookData | map(b => b.pages)
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  = expected: BookData
```

This time the program is fine and the data is wrong, so the error is an
`execute error`. The bad record is rejected as it comes in, before any step
uses it. Every value after that point is a valid `BookData`, as the checker
assumed.

## Open and closed types

A closed type, declared as a `TypeContract`, describes the whole record. A
record with any other field is rejected:

```hql
type Book : TypeContract = {
    title: String
    pages: Int
}

raw : List<Data> = [{title: "Dune", pages: 412, shelf: "sf"}]
raw | Book
```

```text
execute error[contract-violation]: this does not read as Book: field `shelf`: Book declares no such field
 --> <expression>:7:1
  |
7 | raw | Book
  | ^^^^^^^^^^
  = expected: Book
```

An open contract, declared as a `DataContract`, lists only the fields the
program needs. Other fields are allowed and kept:

```hql
type BookData : DataContract = {
    title: String
    pages: Int
}

raw : List<Data> = [{title: "Dune", pages: 412, shelf: "sf"}]
raw | BookData | map(b => b.pages)
```

```text
[412]
```

Use open contracts for data from other systems, which often adds fields you
do not need. Use closed types for values your own program builds, where you
decide the exact shape. [[11-advanced-data-typing/data-contracts|Data contracts]]
covers rules, defaults and validation in detail.

Contracts are built from simpler types: numbers, text, dates and times, and
`Data`. The rest of this chapter covers those, starting in the REPL.

## Trying values in the REPL

`hql repl` reads one input at a time, evaluates it and prints the value. A
binding is kept for the inputs after it, and `:type` asks for a type without
evaluating anything:

```text
$ hql repl
hql 0.5.0. `:help` for commands, `:quit` to leave.
hql> pages = 412
()
hql> pages / 3
137
hql> pages % 3
1
hql> :type pages
Int
hql> title = "Dune"
()
hql> title.upper()
"DUNE"
hql> :type title
String
hql> fmt("{} has {} pages", title, pages)
"Dune has 412 pages"
hql> :quit
```

`412` is an `Int`, so `/` divides whole numbers and drops the remainder, and
`%` gives the remainder. `"Dune"` is a `String`, and `title.upper()` calls a
function of `String` on it. `fmt` writes values into text, one `{}` each.

The same programs run with `hql eval`, as every program in this book does:

```hql
pages = 412
title = "Dune"
fmt("{} has {} pages", title, pages)
```

```text
"Dune has 412 pages"
```

## Type checking

Each operation says which types it takes. An `Int` and a `String` do not add,
and the checker rejects the program before it runs:

```hql
412 + "pages"
```

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: addition requires two Int, two Float or two Decimal operands
 --> <expression>:1:7
  |
1 | 412 + "pages"
  |       ^^^^^^^
```

There is no implicit conversion between types: a function that takes a
`Decimal` does not accept an `Int`. Arithmetic and comparison take an `Int`
and a `Decimal` together, and arithmetic on them gives a `Decimal`; that is
a rule of those operators, not a conversion. A conversion is written: `str`
writes a value as text, and `float` turns an `Int` into a `Float`:

```hql
412.str()
```

```text
"412"
```

`hql check` prints a program's type without evaluating it:

```sh
$ hql check - <<< '412 / 3'
Int
```

## The types, one page each

| Type | What it holds | Page |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Int` | a whole number, 64 bits | [Int](int.hmd) |
| `Float` | a binary floating-point number, written `2.5f` | [Float](float.hmd) |
| `Decimal` | an exact decimal number, written `2.50` | [Decimal](decimal.hmd) |
| `String` | text | [String](string.hmd) |
| `Bool` | `true` or `false` | [Bool](bool.hmd) |
| `Bytes` | a sequence of octets | [Bytes](bytes.hmd) |
| `Date`, `DateTime`, `Instant`, `Duration` | a date, a wall-clock reading, a point in time, a length of time | [Date and time](time.hmd) |
| `Data` | a tree of records, lists and scalars | [Data](data.hmd) |
| `Option<T>` | a `T`, or nothing | [Option](option.hmd) |

Braces write three different things — a set, a `Data` record and, where one
is declared, a map — and [[sets-data-and-maps|sets, Data and maps]] tells
them apart. Lists, sets and maps as collections, and the steps they share, are
[[03-collections|chapter 3]].
